 interaction plots in Figure 6.12 visually depict how the levels of one factor influence the effect of another factor on the response variable.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of the results depicted in Figure 6.12 relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: Each experimental unit must be independent of others.\n2. Normality: The residuals of the model should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance among the groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nInteraction plots are particularly useful when researchers suspect that the effect of one factor on the response variable may depend on the level of another factor. They are applicable in scenarios involving factorial designs, especially when exploring complex interactions in multi-factor experiments.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nTo generate the interaction plots shown in Figure 6.12, researchers typically conduct a factorial ANOVA to assess the significance of main effects and interactions. Following the ANOVA, interaction plots are created by plotting the means of the response variable for each combination of factor levels, allowing for visual assessment of interactions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Interaction plots provide a clear visual representation of how factors interact, making it easier to interpret complex relationships.\n- They help identify significant interactions that may not be evident from main effect analyses alone.\n- The graphical nature of the plots aids in communicating results to stakeholders who may not be familiar with statistical analysis.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Interaction plots can become cluttered and difficult to interpret when too many factors or levels are included.\n- They do not provide quantitative measures of the strength of interactions; statistical tests are still required to confirm significance.\n- Misinterpretation can occur if the 